# Bubbles: one held note is a stream of resonant pings off the grid, and
# nothing sounds between them. A dust fires about seven a second, swelling
# and thinning; each impulse draws the pitch its ping rings at, within an
# octave either side of 880 Hz, reached in a millisecond so the ping before
# it does not click, and a small bend that takes 60 ms to arrive. No impulse
# is heard: it is rounded by a lowpass and struck into a wide bandpass on
# the ping's pitch, which turns it into a few milliseconds of tone, and only
# that rings the two narrow ones after it. So a ping rises in 5 ms and has
# no broadband edge, and two resonances in a row ring twice as long as one.
# Not tuned to the note: any note plays it, and every note plays a different
# stream. Measured on a solo render: an isolated ping rises in 5 ms (10-90%),
# is 20 dB down 31 ms after its peak and 40 dB down at 53 ms; its first 3 ms
# hold 40 dB less above 2.5 times its pitch than at it. Between pings there
# is only their tails: half of all 10 ms stretches are 70 dB or more under
# the pings, and the 40 ms before a ping that follows a gap are silent.
instrument bubbles {
  gain 6
  dust(7.5hz, spread=5db, level=40db) as d
  rand(0.95, glide=1ms) as where
  rand(0.1, glide=60ms) as bend
  wander(0.3hz, 1.5) as tide
  lowpass(400hz, 0.01) as soft
  svf(bandpass, 880hz, 0.1) as strike
  svf(bandpass, 880hz, 0.95) as ping
  svf(bandpass, 880hz, 0.95) as ring
  lowpass(4000hz, 0.01) as round
  adsr(0.005, 0.1, 1, 0.4) as amp
  tide > d.rate
  d > where.draw
  d > bend.draw
  d > soft
  soft > strike
  strike > ping
  ping > ring
  where > strike.cutoff
  bend > strike.cutoff
  where > ping.cutoff
  bend > ping.cutoff
  where > ring.cutoff
  bend > ring.cutoff
  ring > round
  round > amp
  amp > out
}
